{
  "analysis": "We found 152 unique commands from 1 fps sampling, but the game has 262 moves, so we're missing many. Also, many captured commands are partial/intermediate typing states (e.g., `get lam`, `get lamp,clove`). We need to see the full command list and understand the text layout better. Then we likely need higher fps extraction to catch all moves, or a smarter parsing approach.",
  "plan": "Save the full command extraction to a file for review, inspect a few individual OCR files to understand how complete vs partial commands appear, and check CPU count for parallel OCR planning. Then decide whether to extract at higher fps.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 - <<'PY' > /app/commands_1fps.txt\nimport os, re, glob\nfiles = sorted(glob.glob('/app/ocr/frame_*.txt'))\nseen = set()\nfor fp in files:\n    frame = int(re.search(r'frame_(\\d+)', fp).group(1))\n    with open(fp, errors='replace') as f:\n        text = f.read()\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        s = line.strip()\n        m = re.match(r'^>\\s*(.+)$', s)\n        if m:\n            cmd = m.group(1).strip()\n            if cmd and cmd not in seen:\n                seen.add(cmd)\n                print(f'frame {frame:03d}: {cmd}')\nprint(f'\\nTotal unique commands found: {len(seen)}')\nPY\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -80 /app/commands_1fps.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /app/ocr/frame_150.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "nproc\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}
